CRITERION 5 — worker tests assert what they claim                      FAIL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  FAILING ITEM: internal/services/retirement/analysis/one_time_expense_test.go
                func TestOneTimeExpense_HistoricalBacktestPicksItUp (lines 33-59)

  It is the ONLY test guarding the spec clause "historical backtest picks it up",
  and it passes with the feature completely removed from the engine.

  Mutation (rsync copy, real repo untouched): both "+ oneTimeExpenseThisMonth"
  terms deleted from plannedTotalExpenses and totalExpenses in
  internal/services/retirement/engine/stepper.go — i.e. the one-time expense is
  computed and then discarded.

  $ go test -count=1 -run TestOneTimeExpense ./internal/models/ \
      ./internal/services/retirement/... ./internal/handlers/whatif/   [MUTANT]
      --- FAIL: TestOneTimeExpense_MonteCarloPicksItUp
      --- FAIL: TestOneTimeExpense_EngineLoopHitsOnlyItsYear
      (everything else ok)
  $ go test -run 'TestOneTimeExpense|TestCHK_Backtest' -v \
      ./internal/services/retirement/analysis/                          [MUTANT]
      --- PASS: TestOneTimeExpense_HistoricalBacktestPicksItUp     <-- survives
      --- FAIL: TestOneTimeExpense_MonteCarloPicksItUp
                got with=1735591.81 without=1735591.81
      --- FAIL: TestCHK_BacktestSuccessRateDrops (my probe)
                baseline=20.55 with=20.55

  Why it is structurally unreachable, not merely lucky. Its only assertion is
      if with.SurvivedCount > without.SurvivedCount { t.Errorf(...) }
  and in its own scenario:
  $ go test -run TestCHK_WorkerBacktestScenarioNumbers -v ./internal/services/retirement/analysis/
      CHK-5 worker scenario with:    Survived=88 Success=100.0000 MeanFinal=1526127.56 n=88
      CHK-5 worker scenario without: Survived=88 Success=100.0000 MeanFinal=1623975.67 n=88
      CHK-5 mean final-balance delta (without-with) = 97848.11
  Both arms survive 88/88 at 100%, so SurvivedCount can never differ in either
  direction. The assertion cannot fire for any implementation, correct or not.
  The test's own comment (lines 53-54) states the intended check — "must not
  leave the mean final balance unchanged — if it did, the backtest loop dropped
  it" — and the signal is right there (delta 97,848.11) but is never computed or
  asserted on.

  WHAT IS MISSING: one assertion in
  internal/services/retirement/analysis/one_time_expense_test.go, in
  TestOneTimeExpense_HistoricalBacktestPicksItUp, that fails when the backtest
  drops the expense — e.g. mean/aggregate FinalBalance over a.Results strictly
  lower with the expense than without (delta ~97.8k in the existing scenario), or
  a tighter scenario where SuccessRate actually moves (see my CHK-3d: 20.55% ->
  0.00% at PortfolioValue 400k / 2.8k per month / 25 years). The underlying
  behaviour IS correct — my CHK-3d passes on the unmutated tree and fails on the
  mutant — so this is a test defect only, not a product defect.

  ALL OTHER TestOneTimeExpense* TESTS CHECKED AND SUBSTANTIVE:
